#pragma once

// std::tuple for the supported subset (M10).
//
// The shape is a recursive variadic class template: `tuple<int, char>` is
// `{ int head; tuple<char> tail; }`, and `tuple<>` is the empty primary.
//
// Construction is in place rather than through a factory: a factory returning the
// tuple by value is blocked by a front-end gap, not by the target. KR16 now supports
// aggregate returns (ADR 0048) and a hand-written `std::tuple<int, int>` factory
// works on both targets; see the measured limit below.
//
// Element access is named rather than indexed: the free functions `tuple_head` and
// `tuple_tail`, and the members `.head` / `.tail`. Reach element k by chaining members
// (`pair.tail.head`).
//
// `get<I>` is absent, and the reason was measured rather than assumed. What works: a
// non-type template parameter in a declaration (`Fixed<2> f;`) and an explicit non-type
// argument in a call (`at_index<0>(x)`). What does not: a **member function template**
// fails to parse (`expected declarator`), and a qualified use through a template-id with
// a non-type argument fails to parse (`Index<0>::pick`, `expected expression`). Deduction
// of a non-type parameter from an argument is also absent (`cannot deduce the value
// template parameter`). A member `get<I>` needs the first, a free `get<I>` that indexes
// needs the second or recursion over the index.
//
// One limit is real, and it is measured rather than assumed:
//
//   * **An empty tuple cannot be a local.** `tuple<> t;` emits a call to the
//     pattern's constructor and fails to link with `undefined symbol
//     'tuple_ctor_0_value'`. An empty tuple reached as a member is fine --
//     `tuple_size(pair.tail.tail)` is zero -- so the empty type exists and only a
//     local of it is affected. An empty tail built by the element constructor is
//     fine for the same reason.
//
// `make_tuple` is provided. It was blocked by a pack that did not substitute in a
// function template's return type and body; the parser recorded `Tup<Ts...>` in an
// expression context as `Ts ...`, so the pack name carried a trailing space and never
// matched its arity. Fixed on 2026-11-17, together with binding the pack's element types
// (`Ts0`, `Ts1`, ...) and recording a constructor call's resolved type for code
// generation. `make_tuple(1, 2)` and `make_tuple(1, 2, 3)` compile and run on both
// targets.
//
// A mixed-element tuple through the factory still fails: `make_tuple(41, 'a')` reports
// `IR verifier: function 'main': store type does not match its pointer`. Construct such a
// tuple in place -- `tuple<int, char> p(41, 'a');` -- which works.

namespace std {

template <typename... Ts>
struct tuple {
};

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
struct tuple<T, Rest...> {
    T head;
    tuple<Rest...> tail;

    tuple() : head(), tail() {}

    // The element constructor. Its signature carries the class template's pack, so an
    // instance takes one argument per element and `rest...` forwards the remainder to
    // the tail.
    tuple(T first, Rest... rest) : head(first), tail(rest...) {}
};

// The number of elements, for the primary and for every specialization: the pack
// is the whole parameter list, so one function answers both.
template <typename... Ts>
int tuple_size(tuple<Ts...>& value) {
    return sizeof...(Ts);
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
T& tuple_head(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.head;
}

template <typename T, typename... Rest>
tuple<Rest...>& tuple_tail(tuple<T, Rest...>& value) {
    return value.tail;
}

// The by-value factory. Its signature carries the pack in the return type and in the
// parameter list, which is the case a function template's pack substitutes: the instance
// is named from the arguments and its body builds that instance rather than the pattern.
// `make_tuple(1, 'x')` returns `tuple<int, char>`.
template <typename... Ts>
tuple<Ts...> make_tuple(Ts... values) {
    return tuple<Ts...>(values...);
}

}  // namespace std
